CRITERION 4 — render facts on BOTH ranges + sync path in both drag directions.
  MET, with a defect reported below.
  (a) Static render, full page, from my own test:
      CHECKER primary range: <input type="range" id="monthly_living_expenses_input"
        value="7386" aria-valuetext="$7,386" min="0" max="20000" step="100" ...>
      CHECKER mirror  range: <input type="range" value="7386"
        aria-valuetext="$7,386" ... data-quick-adjust-mirror="true" ...>
      Both == "$7,386"; neither contains "$7,400".  formatNumber (render.go:546)
      rounds to whole dollars, so aria-valuetext is byte-identical to the visible
      display span text — the correct WCAG 4.1.2 parity.
  (b) Both drag directions proven by EXECUTING the page's own JS, not by reading
      it: I dumped the rendered page and drove it under jsdom 29.1.1
      (scratchpad/jsdomtest/drive.js), dispatching real input events.
        AFTER LOAD:  primary aria=$7,386  hidden=7386.00  display=$7,386
                     mirror=7386.00/$7,386  phase note=$8,124.60
        DRAG PRIMARY to 7900: primary.value=7900 aria=$7,900 hidden=7900
                     display=$7,900 mirror=7900/$7,900 note=$8,690.00
        DRAG MIRROR to 6500:  aria(primary)=$6,500 hidden=6500 display=$6,500
                     mirror=6500/$6,500 note=$7,150.00
      aria-valuetext is updated on BOTH controls in BOTH directions, in the same
      syncQuickAdjustKey pass that updates the display span. Criterion met.

      DEFECT (reported, not a criterion failure): in the mirror-drag direction
      the primary range's own .value is NOT updated — jsdom output line
      "primary.value = 7900" while "primary aria-valuetext = $6,500". Nothing in
      the tree ever writes that element's value (grep for an assignment to
      #monthly_living_expenses_input returns nothing; it lost
      data-quick-adjust-key in attempt 1, so syncQuickAdjustMirrorControls no
      longer reaches it). Consequence: after a quick-adjust drag, the primary
      slider's thumb/implicit aria-valuenow says 7900 while its aria-valuetext
      announces $6,500, and arrowing that slider jumps back to ~8000. This is
      structural from attempt 1 (which this lane passed); attempt 2 did not cause
      the stale thumb but its new aria-valuetext write makes the disagreement
      audible. Outside criteria 1-5 as written; flagged for the lead /
      checker-a11y / checker-second.

CRITERION 5 — bite test.  Executed; result is a COVERAGE NOTE, per the brief.
  Bite A: deleted the 6-line aria-valuetext block from syncQuickAdjustKey
  (quick-adjust-scripts.html) -> `go test ./...` still exits 0, zero failures.
  No Go test can reach that line; the package has no JS harness. The static
  attribute IS guarded — Bite B: deleted the aria-valuetext line from
  portfolio-settings.html ->
    --- FAIL: TestPortfolioSettingsRender_LivingExpensesPhaseNote/aria-valuetext_...
        living_expenses_phase_note_test.go:222: expected aria-valuetext="$7,386"
  so the initial-render assertion has real teeth on the primary range.
  Bite C (extra, mine): deleted the aria-valuetext line from the quick-adjust
  MIRROR (quick-adjust.html) -> `go test ./...` exits 0, NOTHING fails. The
  mirror's static attribute has no regression guard at all; the worker's new
  render test covers only whatif-portfolio-settings. The package's own
  conventions could reach it (a render test on "whatif-quick-adjust" would),
  so this is a reachable gap, not an inherent one. Weighed per the criteria as
  written: criterion 4 asks that the attribute be PRESENT with the exact value,
  which I proved by command; it does not require a test. Coverage note, not a
  failure. All bite mutations were reverted and the tree re-diffed against the
  real tree before the final run.

